<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ganja, Azerbaijan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Elizavetpol, Kirovabad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ganja is the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, in the west of the country. A major centre of the medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, it was the home of the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizami-ganjavi">Nizami Ganjavi</a> in the 12th century. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>'s death it became the seat of an independent khanate, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> stormed in 1804; renamed Elizavetpol, the city took back the name Ganja in 1918 and, after a Soviet period as Kirovabad, again in 1989.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ganja-azerbaijan.jpg" alt="Ganja, Azerbaijan" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NordNordWest, edited by Toghrul R · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ganja_City_in_Azerbaijan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
